1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,667
This is a walkthrough of NGFW Lua Importer.

2
00:00:03,717 --> 00:00:12,823
It imports verified OpenAppID Lua detectors from the shared library into Cisco FMC, and can activate them as Local App Detectors.

3
00:00:12,873 --> 00:00:17,270
When the program starts, it opens this local page in your browser.

4
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:23,133
The top panel shows the program version, the tested FMC version, and the upload method.

5
00:00:23,183 --> 00:00:30,839
It automates the same upload steps as the FMC web interface, because the public REST API cannot upload custom Lua.

6
00:00:30,889 --> 00:00:35,782
If you run into a compatibility problem, use this email link to report it.

7
00:00:35,832 --> 00:00:42,545
Next, enter the FMC address, username and password. IP addresses are blurred in this video.

8
00:00:42,595 --> 00:00:48,654
Save remembers the address and username. The password stays in memory and is never written to disk.

9
00:00:48,704 --> 00:00:52,368
Test connection logs in to FMC and shows its version.

10
00:00:52,418 --> 00:00:57,512
Search the shared library for the app you need. This demo uses TencentMeeting.

11
00:00:57,562 --> 00:01:04,139
Each row shows the category, risk, business relevance, and the FMC version it was tested on.

12
00:01:04,189 --> 00:01:08,106
Click View Lua to inspect the detector before uploading.

13
00:01:08,156 --> 00:01:09,916
Then close the window.

14
00:01:09,966 --> 00:01:13,436
Select the app, and tick "Activate these Local App Detectors after upload".

15
00:01:13,486 --> 00:01:19,881
The hint explains that activation is pushed to all managed FTDs immediately, with no deploy needed.

16
00:01:19,931 --> 00:01:22,013
Click Upload selected Lua.

17
00:01:22,063 --> 00:01:26,360
Because activation is selected, an impact notice appears first.

18
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:35,203
On Snort 3 devices, Snort is not restarted and traffic is not interrupted. On Snort 2 devices, activation restarts Snort.

19
00:01:35,253 --> 00:01:43,935
Also, traffic that used to be identified as HTTPS will be identified as the new app, which may change which rules it matches.

20
00:01:43,985 --> 00:01:47,822
Tick the acknowledgement, then click Upload and activate.

21
00:01:47,872 --> 00:01:53,261
The upload and activation take one to two minutes. This part is sped up.

22
00:02:00,932 --> 00:02:05,551
When it finishes, the results table shows the app as uploaded and active.

23
00:02:05,601 --> 00:02:18,024
Run log: log in to FMC → download Lua and verify SHA256 → create the app and upload Lua → activate the detector.

24
00:02:18,074 --> 00:02:22,729
Now log in to FMC to confirm the detector was imported.

25
00:02:23,399 --> 00:02:30,630
Because the importer just used the same account, FMC reports an existing session. Choose End Existing Session.

26
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:36,390
Open the Policies menu and choose Advanced → Applications.

27
00:02:38,025 --> 00:02:41,454
Type TencentMeeting into the Name filter.

28
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:52,911
The detection type is CUSTOM, the type is Advanced, and the State switch is on, so the detector is active.

29
00:02:52,961 --> 00:03:00,869
Click edit to see the details: the state is Active, and the detection criteria is the Lua file we just uploaded.

30
00:03:03,895 --> 00:03:07,427
Click Cancel to leave without changes.

31
00:03:10,737 --> 00:03:15,643
The Lua detector is now imported into FMC and active as a Local App Detector.

32
00:03:15,693 --> 00:03:22,342
For compatibility issues, please email yongwli@cisco.com.

